1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,440
Hey everyone, we are going to be looking at repeated games.

2
00:00:02,520 --> 00:00:06,400
Now these are games that are going to be played multiple times.

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:13,640
They can be a finitely repeated game as what we're going to see right here, or they can be an infinitely repeated game.

4
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:18,400
Now in finitely repeated games we do have a set number of rounds.

5
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:22,120
It could be 1, it could be 10, it could be 100, it doesn't really matter.

6
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:25,760
All that we know is that the game will end definitely.

7
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:29,240
So let's have a look at this particular game here.

8
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:38,680
This is between Uber and Lyft and they can choose to invest and innovate into their companies or they can choose to maintain the status quo for Uber.

9
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:42,120
If they invest, they have payoffs of 6 and 4.

10
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:46,600
If Uber decides to maintain, they have payoffs of 7 and 5.

11
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:49,520
And then we get the same thing with Lyft.

12
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:54,280
If Lyft decides to invest, they get payoffs of 6 and 4.

13
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:58,280
And then if they decide to maintain, they get payoffs of 7 and 5.

14
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:01,880
So let's start to look at how this game is going to be played.

15
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:13,240
Now we know in our previous lectures we have a one shot game and we look for the payoffs that dominant strategies that are going to lead to the payoffs each player receives.

16
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:19,080
So one thing that we would do is we would start to look at the strategies and the payoffs of each of those.

17
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:25,120
And we see that the 7 is better for better than 6 for Uber, and the 5 is also better than the 4.

18
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:29,160
So maintaining becomes a dominant strategy for Uber.

19
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:31,760
Let's do the same thing for Lyft.

20
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:36,280
Again, 7 is better than 6 and 5 is better than 4.

21
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:40,400
So again, what we see is a dominant strategy to maintain.

22
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:46,280
That means in this one shot game, the Nash equilibrium is for both of them to maintain.

23
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:49,800
Now let's say that this took place 10 times.

24
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:52,360
So now we're going to look at a 10 round game.

25
00:01:52,480 --> 00:02:00,000
Well, we might naturally assume that they're going to look at the combined payoffs of 5 and 5 and go, but that's only 10.

26
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:06,240
If we both choose to invest, then that's going to be a 6 and 6, which gives us a total of 12.

27
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:12,880
So there may initially be some cooperation in this repeated game of 10 rounds.

28
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:16,360
But what do you think each is going to do in that last round?

29
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:26,280
Well, naturally they're going to say, well, this is the last time that this is going to be played and I can do better than six if I were to go back to maintaining.

30
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:31,600
So in that regard what would take place is you would both again still maintain.

31
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:38,000
So you don't go to the six and six like what you would get in the previous nine rounds.

32
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:42,760
On the final round, you end up with the Nash equilibrium of 5 and 5.

33
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:56,640
So what we see in these finitely repeated games is that we end up reverting back to what the Nash equilibrium is going to be, as opposed to what the socially optimum level of payouts is going to be.

34
00:02:56,800 --> 00:02:59,120
Now, let's look at infinitely repeated games.

35
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:05,600
These have no set end time, no set number of rounds are played indefinitely.

36
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:14,840
Now, with infinitely repeated games, we're going to look at the same type of game here, so the same payoff matrix, everything set up the same.

37
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:22,160
But now we're just going to say, hey, you play this as many times as you want, and there are a few different outcomes that could take place.

38
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:24,320
And we need to look at each of these in turn.

39
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:26,120
Let's start with trigger strategies.

40
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:34,280
These are strategies in which a repeated game where the player stops cooperating to punish another player's break with cooperation.

41
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:48,160
So what we might see is initially that they are going to both choose to maintain, and then if one of them decides to do something else, well, we might say, all right, hey, let's go both invest.

42
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:53,320
But what happens if they're both investing and then one of them decides, you know what?

43
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:57,160
I think I'm going to try to sneak in maintaining, well, guess what?

44
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:00,360
The other player then says, you know what?  We're done.

45
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:01,560
We're done cooperating.

46
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:03,600
We're just going to maintain the rest of the time.

47
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:14,120
And so when they maintain the rest of the time, this now means that the trigger strategy has been employed because one player deviated from the cooperation of the 6 and 6.

48
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:17,320
And so now you're going to be stuck at that Nash equilibrium.

49
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:23,680
So with trigger strategies, your initial move there is to cooperate and get to that 6 and 6.

50
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:29,720
But then if a player deviates from that, then you punish them almost permanently.

51
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:32,960
This doesn't necessarily need to be permanent.

52
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:34,720
It can vary.

53
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:37,440
That is, you might have a forgiving player.

54
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:42,960
They may go back to the cooperative strategy.

55
00:04:43,080 --> 00:04:59,360
And if that's the case, that's great, because now players are doing better in a Pareto optimal outcome, but it could end up being the worst outcome if you continue to punish the player and you don't have any forgiveness in there.

56
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:03,720
Our next one here is going to be the grim strategy.

57
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:08,320
Now, this is a subset of the trigger strategy in which you're going to do the same thing here.

58
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:13,080
A strategy that one player can use to deter Another player from cheating on a cooperative agreement.

59
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:18,960
So let's look at how the grim strategy works and then we'll talk about how to solve the grim strategy.

60
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:30,000
So here you might initially start out with cooperation, and then one player defects and then what we have is the other player says, you know what, we're done.

61
00:05:30,120 --> 00:05:31,920
This is going to be the outcome from now on.

62
00:05:31,960 --> 00:05:35,880
I'm going to maintain, I'm never going to invest, and we're going to end up with these payoffs.

63
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:37,040
That's the grim strategy.

64
00:05:37,080 --> 00:05:41,400
It's actually worse than the trigger strategy because here you're done.

65
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:43,400
There is no forgiveness in the play.

66
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:45,120
You say, this is what's going to happen.

67
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:47,560
We're not going to deviate, we're not going to go back.

68
00:05:47,720 --> 00:05:53,840
So it's a little bit more grim than what the trigger strategy is.

69
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,280
So in that regard, the grim strategy is much more strict.

70
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:08,560
Now, the way that you might could solve the grim strategy problem is to announce at the start of the play, this is the grim strategy.

71
00:06:08,600 --> 00:06:10,320
This is what we're going to do if you deviate.

72
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:16,880
And that may then get the other player to always cooperate and get the better payoffs.

73
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:20,720
Now, the last one that we have here is the tit for tat strategy.

74
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:33,040
With the tit for tat strategy, we are going to begin by cooperating and then we're going to look at what the other player does and we are going to just replicate what they do.

75
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:45,320
So if we start with cooperating six and six and then one player deviates in the second round, then the next player is going to deviate in the third round.

76
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:47,040
They just copy what happens.

77
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:48,480
So they copy what happens.

78
00:06:48,520 --> 00:06:54,200
Now, the nice thing about the tit for tat strategy is that we do start with cooperation.

79
00:06:54,240 --> 00:06:59,040
We do retaliate, but we're much more forgiving in subsequent rounds.

80
00:06:59,200 --> 00:07:03,640
So after the first retaliation, you probably say, okay, okay, we've had enough.

81
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:06,520
Let's go back to what we were doing when we were cooperating.

82
00:07:06,560 --> 00:07:16,080
So the punishment strategy here isn't as strict as the trigger and definitely nowhere near as strict as the grim trigger strategy.

83
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:27,120
So what's going to end up happening is with the tit for tat strategy, you're going to move back to that social, that social or Pareto outcome of where they're both investing.

84
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:34,720
So that's where we end up with we start cooperating, we have a little bit of deviation in some of the rounds, and then we move back to cooperating.

85
00:07:34,840 --> 00:07:37,760
Now, one last thing that we want to talk about is the discount factor.

86
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:45,400
When you look at infinitely repeated games, we're having these players who are basing things on a discount factor.

87
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:49,600
Higher discount factors lead to a higher valuation on future payouts.

88
00:07:49,640 --> 00:08:08,400
So what this essentially means is if these players have high discount factors or their discount rates are high, that is, let's say, if you recall discount rates from present value, they can range anywhere from 0 to 1 and any probability in between, or any, excuse me, any interest rate in between.

89
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:17,320
And that interest rate, the higher it gets, the more you place value on the future payout, then you do the present payout.

90
00:08:17,360 --> 00:08:25,560
So typically, what we're going to see with these high discount factors, people are going to be looking at what is going to be the final outcome.

91
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:34,000
And I'm going to place more weight on that final outcome than I will, you know, the next round or the next round after that, or something like that.

92
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:42,880
So that could easily work in conjunction with that tit for tat strategy, because you're thinking, who really cares if I get, you know, the seven just for that one round?

93
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:47,480
What I really care about is the ultimate payoff in which we're all better off in that regard.

94
00:08:47,640 --> 00:08:51,320
All right, so that is it for our repeated games.

95
00:08:51,480 --> 00:08:53,200
Hope you've enjoyed it, and we'll catch you in our next one.